# Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam
Tamil title: திராவிட முன்னேற்றக் கழகம்

## A Statement by Annadurai

“Let him have committed a thousand mistakes. Is it right, proper or decent for us therefore to keep speaking against him, campaign to abolish his leadership, and remove him from the leader's position?

“We all know his nature. He rebukes people, continually finds fault, and speaks as though he will overturn everything at once. That is his nature. Must we therefore join a struggle to remove him from leadership? Would removal not cause him unbearable pain? Are we to inflict that pain? Let matters go somehow. He says things because of his nature; is it not better to leave him to his own course? We can work in the measure and manner suited to us, abandoning the work of ‘abolishing’ his leadership.

“What matters to us is the doctrine and spreading it through the country. Can we neglect that task while quarrelling with him? Is that becoming of responsible people? If his course is unacceptable, the proper, moral and necessary thing is to put him out of mind, cease working together with him, and independently undertake the work of propagating the principles. Continually citing his leadership and present course as excuses for doing no work is not right.”

Alongside such advice, newspapers and verandah propagandists wrote and spread another version:

“This is Annadurai's conspiracy. He has prepared an elaborate plan, rehearsed it many times, and now marches against Periyar. He wishes to seize the organisation, take its property and dominate it. He plans to join political gamblers. He is cruel, deceitful, base, selfish and conspiratorial. His scheme is to destroy Periyar, and jealousy is its cause.”

Meanwhile orthodox circles and the Congress camp said:

“A sudden crisis has arisen in the Dravidar Kazhagam. A split has opened its mouth. They will attack and destroy one another. The principles spread through the country by that organisation will no longer spread, so our dominance will cease trembling and gain new life.”

A few like me, who preferred quiet and an independent course, advised: “Why bear this burden and dispute? Withdraw separately. Do what you can from there; if you cannot, be glad to see others do it.”

Assailed by these several waves of opinion, I had to spend many days thinking and consulting about the problems.

Had the issue been only to reach a decision for myself, it would have ended easily and quickly. I would have withdrawn; that is familiar to me. This time I had to decide on behalf of a very large body of comrades. Amid approximately five currents of thought, I had to make a responsible decision that both reflected and shaped the views of thousands. This was no ordinary problem. It was the second great decision of my life.

In 1935 I made my first: after graduating, I would not seek employment but work in public life. From that year until 1949 I had no need for another such decision. The path and destination were known, and only the journey remained. Though difficulties and disturbances arose during those fourteen years, they were neither of the magnitude nor kind that required changing that first choice.

Following the poetic line, “This heart exists to bear anything,” I endured them. Had my connection with the movement and Periyar been ordinary, I would have fled at the first slight disturbance. But the attachment and affection created by fourteen years were not light. Had my mind been easily tempted, I would have crossed from party to party. Because I had neither such temptation nor desire for office, I remained in this party despite every blow and hardship, in the front rank when possible and beside the fence when it was not.

At times I withdrew because of a difference. Some comrades immediately alleged that Congress had bought me and that I dreamt of becoming a minister. When I returned to active leadership, the same people praised me: “If our Anna desired office, would not a ministry come looking for him? Imagine the high position he would hold if he were in Congress!” The praise meant nothing beyond affection; the hurried condemnation meant nothing beyond groundless hostility. That is how I usually consoled myself.

I never once thought of running to another party. I sometimes thought a party unnecessary, that public service could be done without one, and even that public life itself was unnecessary. But I never thought of deserting to another party. Bearing five currents of opinion, I had to weigh each proposal and reach a sound decision that would not injure the principles. My mind became a furnace; thought had difficult work to do.

I stood at a major stage in life. Knowing my decision concerned not only me but many thousands increased the responsibility. My place in the Dravidar Kazhagam was not one of hunger that another party could satisfy. Many others held even more important places. Central figures and friends who had worked with the leader longer than I had were compelled to consider a new course. Could people connected to an organisation for ten years easily, pleasantly or quickly sever it? I had served fourteen years. Some friends had left Congress with Periyar after waiting with him at the Kanchipuram conference. Others worked in Congress while he did, then in the party he later established. All now had to make a new decision.

The bond between the leader and workers in the Dravidar Kazhagam was unlike that in other parties. There was affection and, although contrary to Self-Respect doctrine, a kind of devotion. No one would deny it; they would proudly admit it. Thus, unlike disputes elsewhere that instantly sever bonds and begin attacks, ours produced tears. When Achariyar and Kamaraj fought in Congress and marshalled their forces, neither could have wept. We truly did, not merely in words. Because no other party had this relationship, I needed time to decide at this crisis, enough time to test many people's patience.

Had my mind contained the rash plan of abusing him and leaving, I need not have shed so many tears or delayed so long. Had it contained treachery, I would have used this as a pretext to run to another party. Because I possessed neither rashness nor treacherous intent, I remained in distress for more than three months.

Without trying to discover what decision I might reach, some supporters offered predictions of every kind. Some did so honestly; others acted as though this opportunity, once missed, would never return. Their propaganda followed the old style, unsuited to the present but the only style they knew. They claimed a mighty planned attempt was under way to march against Periyar and seize his leadership.

While my friends and I had reached no decision, remained in anguish and asked the leader why he had reduced us to this condition, that campaign proceeded through newspapers, pamphlets and messengers. At that point our resolutions and statements said only that unless the marriage was stopped, we could not work under his leadership. No one attempted discussion to remove the difficulty.

During the recent German war, the Nazis used a new kind of explosive that made a terrifying noise. On hearing it, people screamed and fled, though it killed no one and demolished no building. It merely created panic and was called a “screaming bomb.”

In the same fashion, those crowning him shouted: “Alas, what injustice! They are leaping upon Periyar and plan to overthrow him! Come together immediately and avert the danger!” They catalogued his ability, service, and benefits to country and people, and emotionally chanted his virtues, all to create fear that a great assault was planned. Why? By making that claim first, they could later mock, “What were these people able to accomplish?” They threw the screaming bomb for this petty satisfaction.

The marriage question caused my friends and me to condemn Periyar's course, with reasons and sincere conviction. Some waited for the condemnation to burn itself out. They issued general statements called explanations and clarifications, without success. As criticism grew daily, they decided to push into the background the marriage that had caused it and instead teach the organisation what kind of people its critics supposedly were. Accusations were loaded upon us.

Even in an ordinary court, an accused sometimes files a counter-case against the complainant; it is not considered strong. In public life too, discerning people see such a counter-case as evidence of weakness.

The published criticism was that Periyar's course concerning the marriage was wrong, contrary to principle, likely to lower the organisation in public esteem, destructive of democracy, and damaging to comrades' self-respect. Even if these were treated as charges, the proper response was explanation and rebuttal. Asking instead, “Do you know what sort of people make these charges?” was neither honest nor relevant.

Only after we criticised the marriage and his subsequent course were we accused of having long been traitors. Could that fit the facts? It may delight some comrades, but the public judgment is plain: “The leader married; members said it was wrong; unable to reconcile his critics, he accuses them of all manner of things to conceal his own fault.”

The counter-case contains mutually contradictory and meaningless accusations:

1. “I had already planned to drive all these people out, so they fled before I could.”

2. “Their departure benefits the organisation; a malign burden has gone.”

3. “By leaving separately, these rebels weaken the organisation.”

4. “They seek to capture it and involve it in elections.”

5. “They seek to capture it with a secret desire to fraternise with Congress.”

6. “The organisation is labelled anti-Brahmin, and they flee in fear of that label.”

7. “Congress itself incites them.”

8. “When they learnt I would not let the organisation contest Congress in elections, they left because they desired somehow to enter the legislature.”

9. “It is good that these people are gone.”

10. “Let them run a separate organisation; if honest, perhaps they too can do something.”

11. “Their separate organisation is destructive work and a conspiracy to abolish the Dravidar Kazhagam.”

12. “They pretend to accept the principles but reject the leadership. Even if they cannot accept the leader, should they not still work for the principles?”

13. “Those who reject the leadership and leave should invent new principles. Who are they to speak of the old ones?”

14. “The Dravidar Kazhagam is about to enter an intense struggle and fill the prisons; they flee from fear.”

15. “They speak of struggle and radical plans to gain publicity for the new organisation and the names ‘martyr’ and ‘hero.’ Their plan is a conspiracy instigated by the government.”

16. “The government plans to destroy the Dravidar Kazhagam by arresting and imprisoning Periyar over the Udumalai case and the Island Grounds speech. Choosing that very moment, the conspirators move to capture the organisation while Periyar goes to prison crying ‘Alas!’”

17. “To prevent wealth from reaching the Dravidar Kazhagam, the government deliberately withdrew an existing case against Periyar.”

Thus the contradictions coil around one another. If it is true that he intended to expel us, why think endlessly about those who have gone and perform daily “worship” over them? If the new organisation is a government creation, how can it also be true that it plans to contest elections against Congress?

If the government's attempt to prosecute and imprison Periyar is a plan to destroy the Dravidar Kazhagam, then a government plan to arrest me and others in the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam must likewise be an effort to destroy the new body at birth. How then can that same act be dismissed as a government device to manufacture a rebel leader, hero and martyr?

Each claim is meaningless alone, inconsistent and contradictory beside the others, and together they form a written screen designed to hide the original charge: a counter-case. That is why we refuse to worry about them.

Only after the deepest deliberation did we decide to form a separate organisation called the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam and work through it. Seeing the extent and character of its support produces anger and grief among opponents, and denunciation rolls forth. There may even be plans to sow rebellion. We need not worry. Purity in our hearts is enough. Our thought is unchained, a great opportunity. Our conduct carries no smell of selfishness, so there is no reason to falter. We have lost nothing, so why panic? We expected nothing and were not disappointed, so why tremble?

I call upon Dravidian comrades to establish Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam branches everywhere and undertake the work of spreading sound knowledge. I thank those who have already shown enthusiasm and entered the work.

Cast away the spirit of factional conflict. When condemned, do not let the words enter your ears; even if they bore their way in, do not let them remain in your heart. Not everyone will mistakenly imagine that affection for him must be demonstrated by anger toward us. A few seekers of opportunity may do so. I firmly believe others will possess the dignity expressed by the Dindigul comrade who said, “I remain with Periyar, but I am not an enemy of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam.”

Comrade Tiruchi Vedachalam wrote days ago that an irreparable split had arisen in the Dravidar Kazhagam, forming Periyar's party and the others' party; that he belonged to Periyar's, but both would labour for the separation of Dravida Nadu and it was therefore good for both to work. Many will possess such character. For a few, petty behaviour has become their way of life. Do not anxiously attend to their angry words. Attend to the country's condition and come forward to perform our work effectively.

Remember that most of us who stood in the Dravidar Kazhagam's front rank are together in one camp. There is no benefit in spending all this strength opposing a few opportunists. We must attend not to them, but to the cruelties eating the country and the social degradations against which we must wage a severe struggle. We possess abundant strength and now have the opportunity. Realise this and come to work.

I invite everyone wishing to join the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam. This second decision of my life rests not upon my individual strength but upon collective labour. However irrationally and causelessly we who stand together are criticised today, he himself once called us “commanders, gems and precious stones.” We called him Periyar then and have not forgotten that affection or lost our civility today. He now calls us “malign influences.” Never mind. Do not worry over his finding fault with us. If blaming us gives his mind peace and contentment, let that desire exhaust itself.

Instead, observe how contemptuously orthodox perversions and fascist fanatics think and speak about us, Dravidian society and the country. Strive to remove that degradation. There is abundant work there. Let us undertake it and not spend thought upon trifles.

Annadurai

Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 2 October 1949.

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is retained verbatim. The source contains broken lines, stray page furniture, character substitutions, and damaged numbering in its catalogue of accusations. The English uses continuous editorial numbering to preserve all recoverable accusations in source order; it does not claim to reproduce the printed numerals. One OCR-corrupted English label for the wartime “screaming bomb” is translated from context rather than reproduced. Names and institutional terms, including Periyar, Dravidar Kazhagam, Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam, Self-Respect, Achariyar, Kamaraj, Udumalai and Island Grounds, are rendered conservatively. The source's polemical descriptions are preserved as historical language, not independently endorsed.
